# GitLab Flavored Markdown

When writing text GitLab will render (issues, merge requests, comments, wiki, milestones, releases, or Markdown in this repo), use GitLab Flavored Markdown: https://docs.gitlab.com/user/markdown/

## References

These shorthand references become links. They do not work inside inline code, code blocks, or Markdown snippet files.

| Target | This project | Another project |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Issue | `#123` | `namespace/project#123` |
| Merge request | `!123` | `namespace/project!123` |
| Epic | `&123` or `[epic:123]` | `group&123` |
| Label | `~bug` or `~"feature request"` | `namespace/project~bug` |
| Milestone | `%v1.0` or `%"release candidate"` | `namespace/project%v1.0` |
| Snippet | `$123` | `namespace/project$123` |
| Commit | `9ba12248` (8–40 hex digits) | `namespace/project@9ba12248` |
| Commit range | `9ba12248...b19a04f5` | `namespace/project@9ba12248...b19a04f5` |
| User, group, team | `@user`, `@group`, `@all` | |
| Wiki page | `[[Page title]]` | `[wiki_page:namespace/project:Page title]` |

- A merge request is `!123`. `#123` is an issue (or, in a group, sometimes an epic).
- A milestone is `%title` or `%"Title With Spaces"`. Never write `Milestone #3` — `#3` links to issue `#3`, not milestone iid 3.
- Same namespace, other project: `project!123` or `project#123` (project slug only).
- Append `+` to include the title (`!123+`) or `+s` for the summary (`#123+s`).
- Escape a literal with a backslash: `\#123`, `\!123`, `\~bug`, `\%v1.0`.
- Issue and merge request titles allow only code spans, emoji, autolinks, and these references.

## Other GLFM the agent should prefer

- Task lists: `- [ ]`, `- [x]`, and `- [~]` (inapplicable).
- Table of contents, on its own line in a description or wiki: `[[_TOC_]]`.
- Alerts: `> [!note]`, `> [!tip]`, `> [!important]`, `> [!caution]`, `> [!warning]`.
- Diagrams in ` ```mermaid ` or ` ```plantuml ` fences.
